WEBVTT

00:00:00.714 --> 00:00:02.472
Find related papers that an article could

00:00:02.492 --> 00:00:04.611
have cited but did not.

00:00:04.786 --> 00:00:06.982
The chapter’s probe requires identifying

00:00:07.002 --> 00:00:09.804
the article, extracting its references,

00:00:09.825 --> 00:00:12.253
finding related work, and comparing

00:00:12.273 --> 00:00:14.392
identities. Would running more searches

00:00:14.412 --> 00:00:16.466
alone complete that task?

00:00:16.642 --> 00:00:19.714
Pause and identify the missing operation.

00:00:22.149 --> 00:00:24.556
The comparison must actually happen.

00:00:24.732 --> 00:00:27.959
Our invented set diagram removes already

00:00:27.979 --> 00:00:30.240
cited identities from a related candidate

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.830
pool. The remaining records are candidates

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.420
for checking, not verified citation

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.234
omissions; timing, relevance and metadata

00:00:39.294 --> 00:00:40.575
still need assessment.

00:00:41.481 --> 00:00:43.556
The author’s December twenty twenty five

00:00:43.576 --> 00:00:46.121
probe reports Asta stopping at the target

00:00:46.141 --> 00:00:48.698
lookup, and Consensus returning already

00:00:48.718 --> 00:00:51.674
cited papers despite four sub agents.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:54.355
SciSpace attempted reference extraction

00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:56.572
and comparison; most suggestions were

00:00:56.592 --> 00:00:59.922
uncited, but metadata caused some matching

00:00:59.942 --> 00:01:02.783
failures and completion was unclear.

00:01:03.324 --> 00:01:06.161
General assistants also depended on access

00:01:06.181 --> 00:01:07.539
to the full text.

00:01:07.715 --> 00:01:10.259
This was one task type, not a general

00:01:10.279 --> 00:01:11.612
capability ranking.

00:01:11.787 --> 00:01:14.421
Passing or failing it does not establish

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:17.746
robust agency across tasks or identify a

00:01:17.766 --> 00:01:19.356
failure’s sole cause.

00:01:20.262 --> 00:01:22.142
A trace records what the system did.

00:01:22.305 --> 00:01:24.437
It does not reveal every action it could

00:01:24.457 --> 00:01:26.988
have taken. Missing reference extraction

00:01:27.008 --> 00:01:29.874
or source access can block the task.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:32.624
MCP can expose search operations to a

00:01:32.644 --> 00:01:35.846
controller: their inputs, outputs and

00:01:35.866 --> 00:01:39.338
purpose. Scite and Wiley AI Gateway

00:01:39.358 --> 00:01:41.335
provide examples in the chapter.

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.286
The underlying retrieval algorithm stays

00:01:44.306 --> 00:01:45.072
separate.

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:48.554
Wiley’s documented server returns relevant

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:51.196
full text chunks with paper metadata.

00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:53.915
Scite documents tools and Search API

00:01:53.935 --> 00:01:56.299
filters, but does not document ranking

00:01:56.319 --> 00:01:58.632
parity with its web interface; its

00:01:58.652 --> 00:02:01.492
Assistant is not offered through MCP.

00:02:02.051 --> 00:02:03.967
The controller chooses when to call those

00:02:03.987 --> 00:02:06.905
operations, what to ask and whether to

00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:09.843
search again. The exposed operations,

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:12.240
authentication and library entitlements

00:02:12.260 --> 00:02:15.191
still apply. An endpoint does not provide

00:02:15.211 --> 00:02:17.910
every interface feature or a route around

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:18.863
a subscription.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.944
The chapter illustrates a hypothetical

00:02:21.964 --> 00:02:25.461
search about open access and citations.

00:02:25.624 --> 00:02:27.846
Round one returns papers about overall

00:02:27.866 --> 00:02:30.513
citation differences, while selection bias

00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:31.995
methods are missing.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.031
Later searches follow the returned

00:02:34.051 --> 00:02:37.343
terminology and references, reinforcing

00:02:37.363 --> 00:02:38.631
the same aspect.

00:02:39.181 --> 00:02:41.823
After three rounds the budget ends, with

00:02:41.843 --> 00:02:45.019
selection bias methods still unexplored.

00:02:45.194 --> 00:02:47.687
These are hypothetical recorded actions

00:02:47.707 --> 00:02:50.316
and observations, not private model

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.274
reasoning. The example shows how a

00:02:52.294 --> 00:02:54.736
shortlist bottleneck can recur and

00:02:54.756 --> 00:02:56.449
influence the next search.

00:02:57.365 --> 00:02:59.764
An alternative second action explicitly

00:02:59.784 --> 00:03:02.419
searches for open access, citation

00:03:02.439 --> 00:03:05.047
advantage and selection bias methods.

00:03:05.222 --> 00:03:07.109
Suppose that action retrieves

00:03:07.129 --> 00:03:09.197
methodological papers absent from the

00:03:09.217 --> 00:03:12.367
first round. The cumulative candidate pool

00:03:12.387 --> 00:03:15.524
can then expand, informing later choices.

00:03:16.066 --> 00:03:18.485
The task brief, a librarian or the

00:03:18.505 --> 00:03:20.353
controller could prompt that action.

00:03:20.528 --> 00:03:23.342
It is not guaranteed to occur or succeed.

00:03:23.517 --> 00:03:25.972
Citation searching does not always stay

00:03:25.992 --> 00:03:28.897
inside one aspect; the illustration

00:03:28.917 --> 00:03:31.706
contrasts possible trajectories, not

00:03:31.726 --> 00:03:33.226
universal outcomes.

00:03:34.114 --> 00:03:36.262
A workflow may stop because its steps

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:39.109
ended. A harness may impose a round limit

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:41.673
or budget, or the user may interrupt.

00:03:41.823 --> 00:03:44.303
A model may instead decide that the

00:03:44.323 --> 00:03:46.970
evidence it has seen is sufficient.

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:49.817
That last decision applies a relevance

00:03:49.837 --> 00:03:52.962
criterion to results the user may not have

00:03:52.982 --> 00:03:54.804
inspected. Record it.

00:03:54.966 --> 00:03:57.485
A budget reached, declining yield or a

00:03:57.505 --> 00:03:59.753
model saying enough does not prove

00:03:59.773 --> 00:04:02.201
completeness, and two runs may stop

00:04:02.221 --> 00:04:03.026
differently.

00:04:03.951 --> 00:04:06.335
Evaluating the loop requires the available

00:04:06.355 --> 00:04:10.420
action menu as well as the executed trace,

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.456
intermediate results and stopping

00:04:12.476 --> 00:04:15.485
conditions. Otherwise an access limit can

00:04:15.505 --> 00:04:17.804
look like reasoning failure, and an early

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:20.060
omission can disappear behind a fluent

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:21.167
final answer.

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.441
More rounds add latency and tokens.

00:04:24.578 --> 00:04:27.560
A known procedure may be cheaper and more

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.292
reproducible when prescribed in advance.

00:04:30.454 --> 00:04:33.037
Choose the arrangement for the task, and

00:04:33.057 --> 00:04:34.996
evaluate what it recovered and what it

00:04:35.016 --> 00:04:36.632
failed to explore.
